Post-Infectious IBS Mast Cell Overlap
Complex Fatigue Entities
Persistent gut dysfunction following gastroenteritis

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CFE-036
Reason for Visit: Urgent evaluation of intractable abdominal pain and severe diarrhea to rule out acute bowel obstruction or severe infectious enterocolitis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a sudden exacerbation of severe, unrelenting abdominal cramping and multiple episodes of watery diarrhea, leading to presyncope. Immediate physical assessment was mandated to rule out an acute surgical abdomen, evaluate hydration status, and administer acute antispasmodic therapy to prevent inevitable emergency department presentation before standard clinic hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My stomach is tied in knots, I can't leave the toilet, and I'm so exhausted I feel faint.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports an acute flare of chronic bowel issues that originally began 6 months ago after a severe bout of food poisoning. The current episode started yesterday evening with marked bloating, progressing linearly over 12 hours to severe visceral hypersensitivity, unpredictable explosive diarrhea, and profound systemic fatigue. The patient states even drinking a sip of water triggers intense, painful spasms throughout the entire abdomen.
Functional Status: Confined to the bathroom; unable to stand without severe dizziness.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high risk of orthostatic falls secondary to fluid loss and exhaustion.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/70 mmHg | HR: 108 bpm | Temp: 36.9 C | RR: 18 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Pale, visibly uncomfortable, clutching abdomen, dry mucous membranes.
Physical Exam: Abdomen is distended but soft; diffuse tenderness present without focal peritonism, guarding, or rigidity. Hyperactive bowel sounds auscultated in all four quadrants. Rectal exam deferred. Hemodynamics demonstrate mild postural tachycardia consistent with volume depletion. Clinical presentation is typical for persistent gut dysfunction following gastroenteritis overlapping with localized mast cell reactivity.
Environmental Observations: Easy access to bathroom maintained. Unopened water bottles nearby.
Medication Reconciliation: No current active medications. Attempted to take over-the-counter loperamide but vomited immediately due to nausea.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Post-Infectious IBS Mast Cell Overlap - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Appendicitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Lack of focal right lower quadrant pain, negative Rovsing's sign, and absence of rebound tenderness.

Infectious Enterocolitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of fever, lack of sick contacts, and classical rapid post-prandial onset mirroring previous identical non-infectious flares.

Bowel Obstruction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Continuous passage of stool and flatus, with a soft, non-rigid abdomen.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's acute presentation of agonizing abdominal pain, severe diarrhea, and presyncope warranted an immediate after-hours home visit to definitively exclude a surgical abdomen and assess for dangerous hypovolemia. With acute peritonitis ruled out, the patient required urgent administration of parenteral antispasmodics and antiemetics. Deferring care until normal hours was clinically unsafe, as the massive fluid shifts and unremitting pain would have inevitably resulted in syncope or a costly, distressing ambulance transfer.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Hyoscine Butylbromide (Buscopan) 20mg IM to halt gastrointestinal smooth muscle spasm and Ondansetron 4mg sublingually for nausea. Prescribed Amitriptyline 10mg nocte for visceral pain modulation. Targeted intervention strategy includes PEA, Zinc, and Amitriptyline.
Positive Behavioral Change: By strictly consuming only clear, room-temperature electrolyte fluids for the next 24 hours, you will actively rest the hyper-reactive nerves in your gut lining, directly preventing further violent spasms and rewarding yourself with immediate, sustained relief from the cramping.
Home-Based Services Optional: None acutely.
DME/Safety Optional: None required.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3 days to establish long-term neuromodulatory and mast-cell stabilizing therapies.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department via 000 if you begin vomiting blood, pass large amounts of dark blood in your stool, or if your abdomen becomes suddenly rock-hard and agonizing to touch.

